1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,260
Chapter twelve.

2
00:00:01,260 --> 00:00:04,680
What the past tells you about the future.

3
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:09,100
In chapter seven, knowing X lowered the entropy of Y, on average.

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,470
That drop is the mutual information.

5
00:00:11,470 --> 00:00:13,710
It's the bits that knowing one thing saves you

6
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:16,180
when you guess the other, and it's symmetric.

7
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,660
It's also a divergence, of the joint distribution

8
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:21,390
from independence, so it's never negative.

9
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:25,390
In our books, the next letter carries 4.08 bits;

10
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:28,580
knowing the letter before it, 3.33.

11
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,910
So neighbouring letters share 0.75 bits.

12
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:34,630
Two letters back share 1.36,

13
00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:37,750
though the second one on its own shares only 0.27:

14
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:41,410
together, letters say more than apart.

15
00:00:41,853 --> 00:00:43,563
Now process what you know.

16
00:00:43,563 --> 00:00:48,313
Suppose Z is computed from Y, by any function at all, even a noisy one.

17
00:00:48,313 --> 00:00:52,473
The chain rule splits what Y and Z together say about X in two ways:

18
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:55,793
first Y, then whatever Z adds, which is nothing.

19
00:00:55,793 --> 00:00:58,533
Or first Z, then whatever Y adds.

20
00:00:58,533 --> 00:01:02,843
Compare the two, and Z can never tell you more about X than Y did.

21
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:06,743
No amount of processing creates information.

22
00:01:07,153 --> 00:01:09,203
Let's watch it happen in our books.

23
00:01:09,203 --> 00:01:11,443
Keep only the kind of the previous letter:

24
00:01:11,443 --> 00:01:13,843
space, vowel or consonant.

25
00:01:13,843 --> 00:01:17,893
Of the 0.75 bits, 0.35 survive.

26
00:01:17,893 --> 00:01:19,013
Or garble it:

27
00:01:19,013 --> 00:01:21,763
replace a share of the letters with random ones.

28
00:01:21,763 --> 00:01:24,663
The more noise, the less it tells, down to nothing.

29
00:01:24,663 --> 00:01:26,823
Do both, and you lose more:

30
00:01:26,823 --> 00:01:28,923
each curve stays under the one before.

31
00:01:28,923 --> 00:01:30,843
A law for learning machines too:

32
00:01:30,843 --> 00:01:33,403
whatever a network computes from its context can't

33
00:01:33,403 --> 00:01:37,303
know more about the next token than the context itself.

34
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:39,707
How far back does that reach?

35
00:01:39,707 --> 00:01:42,887
Measure pairs of letters at distance d across our books.

36
00:01:42,887 --> 00:01:46,937
These are finite-sample estimates, with an approximate bias correction.

37
00:01:46,937 --> 00:01:50,587
The neighbouring letter estimate is 0.75 bits.

38
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:53,467
At our largest measured distance, 1000 letters,

39
00:01:53,467 --> 00:01:57,227
the corrected estimate is about 7.32 millionths of a bit;

40
00:01:57,227 --> 00:01:58,987
one shuffled version of the same letters

41
00:01:58,987 --> 00:02:02,347
gives about minus 0.69 millionths of a bit.

42
00:02:02,347 --> 00:02:05,227
The shuffled control is a reference realization,

43
00:02:05,227 --> 00:02:07,097
not an uncertainty interval.

44
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:08,987
Both axes are logarithmic.

45
00:02:08,987 --> 00:02:11,467
Nonpositive estimates cannot appear on them;

46
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:13,387
their signed values are retained,

47
00:02:13,387 --> 00:02:15,587
including the control shown at the right.

48
00:02:15,587 --> 00:02:17,887
A fit is a summary, not a law.

49
00:02:17,887 --> 00:02:20,287
The gold line is a descriptive least-squares fit to the

50
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:23,407
positive estimates from twenty to one thousand letters.

51
00:02:23,407 --> 00:02:25,897
It is not a test that proves a power law.

52
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:27,657
Bias correction is approximate,

53
00:02:27,657 --> 00:02:30,247
especially for overlapping pairs from text.

54
00:02:30,247 --> 00:02:33,127
Corrected estimates can fluctuate below zero,

55
00:02:33,127 --> 00:02:35,907
even though true mutual information is nonnegative.

56
00:02:35,907 --> 00:02:38,307
Our measurement stops at this distance.

57
00:02:38,307 --> 00:02:40,787
It does not establish what happens beyond it,

58
00:02:40,787 --> 00:02:43,377
or prove that memory helps at every distance.

59
00:02:43,377 --> 00:02:46,257
Long context remains a question for models to test:

60
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:47,697
attention, in Part 13.
